WEBVTT

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:27.520
<v Ira Glass>7:00 AM, Tijuana, Mexico, at a little plaza just outside the border crossing into the United States. About 250 people, lots with backpacks and suitcases, lots with kids, crowd around a woman who holds an oversized notebook in her arms. That's what I've come to see. The notebook.
<v Ira Glass>上午7点，墨西哥蒂华纳，美国边境检查站外的一个小广场上。大约250人，许多人背着背包、拖着行李箱，还有很多孩子，他们围住了一名怀抱超大笔记本的女子。我就是为了一睹这一幕而来，那个笔记本。

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:31.440
<v Karen>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Karen>[讲西班牙语]

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.310
<v Ira Glass>Anybody else have 532?
<v Ira Glass>还有谁是532号吗？

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:36.470
<v Karen>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Karen>[讲西班牙语]

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:38.680
<v Ira Glass>Anybody got 533?
<v Ira Glass>有人拿到533号吗？

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:41.670
<v Karen>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Karen>[讲西班牙语]

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:53.530
<v Ira Glass>534? The notebook is a list of names, each neatly written in ballpoint pen. If she calls you, then you get to enter the official port of entry. It's just over the fence. Each number has 10 names under it.
<v Ira Glass>534号？这个笔记本是一份名单，每一个名字都用圆珠笔工整地写在上面。如果她叫到你的名字，你就能进入官方入境口岸。就在围栏那边。每个号码下有10个名字。

00:00:53.530 --> 00:01:00.360
<v Karen>OK. [SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Karen>好的。[讲西班牙语]

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:12.780
<v Ira Glass>You have to get in the book. If you just walk up to the port of entry and say you want asylum in the United States, you're running for your life, they'll tell you to get out. You have to go outside. Get a number. Wait your turn. A couple of people told us this happened to them.
<v Ira Glass>你必须得记在那个本子上。如果你只是径直走到入境口岸，说你想在美国寻求庇护，说你在逃避生命危险，他们会叫你离开。你必须去外面，拿个号码，等待轮到你。有几个人告诉我们，他们就是这样被对待的。

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:14.220
<v Karen>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Karen>[讲西班牙语]

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:24.870
<v Ira Glass>She starts to call out all the names under the number 535. I've edited out the last names here because so many people were nervous about being identified by cartels and gangs when we talked to them.
<v Ira Glass>她开始点535号下的名字。我在这里编辑掉了姓氏，因为当我们和他们交谈时，许多人担心被贩毒集团和帮派认出来。

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:35.060
<v Karen>[SPEAKING SPANISH] Alexa? Alexa? Alberto?
<v Karen>[讲西班牙语] Alexa？Alexa？Alberto？

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:52.650
<v Ira Glass>She's standing outside the busiest border crossing in the Western hemisphere, a complex of buildings so sprawling that I never figured out exactly where they started and ended. It's run by a federal agency with 60,000 employees. This woman is not one of them. She's seeking asylum herself, here with her son.
<v Ira Glass>她站在西半球最繁忙的边境口岸外，那一带建筑群非常庞大，我始终没搞清它们从哪开始、在哪结束。这由一个拥有6万名员工的联邦机构管理。这名女子并不是其中一员。她自己也是寻求庇护者，带着儿子在这里。

00:01:52.650 --> 00:02:09.830
<v Ira Glass>She said her husband was killed by gangs back in El Salvador. Then in July, they threatened to do it to her 10-year-old son, too, if she didn't pay up. So she said she quit her job and abandoned her house and came here. That's who we have pulling people from the crowd and lining them up to enter the United States. That's our system for doing it these days.
<v Ira Glass>她说她的丈夫在萨尔瓦多被帮派杀害了。然后在7月份，他们威胁说，如果她不交钱，也要对她10岁的儿子下毒手。所以她说她辞了职，弃了房，来到这里。这就是我们现在安排在人群中整理队伍进入美国的人。这就是我们目前处理这件事的体系。

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:13.850
<v Karen>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Karen>[讲西班牙语]

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:30.920
<v Ira Glass>Calm down. Calm down, she says. Now, in theory, this job shouldn't fall to a random person who's not even a US citizen. Under US law and international treaties, the moment anybody seeking asylum presents him or herself to a border patrol officer, that officer has an obligation to process them, right then.
<v Ira Glass>冷静点。冷静点，她说。按理说，这项工作本不该落在一个连美国公民都不是的普通人身上。根据美国法律和国际条约，任何寻求庇护者向边境巡逻人员出示身份的瞬间，该人员就有义务当场处理他们的申请，对吧？

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:53.840
<v Ira Glass>So in theory, all the people standing in this plaza should be able to march up to the huge border station and get processed immediately. No wait, no notebook with numbers. But under President Trump, the government's saying it doesn't have the capacity to process all the people showing up at our southern border. So people stand outside our border, waiting. Not just here, but at official ports of entry all across the southwest.
<v Ira Glass>所以理论上，站在这个广场上的所有人本应能直接走向庞大的边境站并立即得到处理。不用等待，也不需要那个写着号码的笔记本。但在特朗普总统治下，政府称其没有能力处理出现在南部边境的所有人。所以人们只能站在边境之外等待。不仅是在这里，在整个西南部的官方入境口岸都是如此。

00:02:53.840 --> 00:03:17.120
<v Ira Glass>Over the last six months, Customs and Border Protection officers have been stopping asylum seekers from even walking up to federal facilities at the border. This has been filmed by crews from CBS, NBC, Fox, and many others. They stop people in the middle of bridges that cross from Mexico into the United States at El Paso and Brownsville, and turn them away before they can even get to the official ports of entry on the other side to apply for asylum.
<v Ira Glass>过去六个月里，海关及边境保护局的官员一直在阻止寻求庇护者走到边境的联邦设施前。哥伦比亚广播公司（CBS）、美国全国广播公司（NBC）、福克斯（Fox）等多家媒体的摄制组都拍下了这一幕。他们在埃尔帕索和布朗斯维尔从墨西哥进入美国的桥梁中间拦住人们，在他们到达另一边的官方入境口岸申请庇护之前就把他们赶走。

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:40.880
<v Ira Glass>Customs and Border Protection says the people are allowed in once space becomes available. They expect this will be temporary, and I quote, "No one is being denied the opportunity to make a claim of credible fear or seek asylum." The lawyers for the immigrants point out that, in the past, these facilities have not had a problem processing the number of people who show up today. So people are kept waiting outside to fend for themselves.
<v Ira Glass>海关及边境保护局称，一旦有空间，人们就会被允许进入。他们预计这只是暂时的，我引用原话：“没有人被拒绝提供陈述受迫害恐惧或寻求庇护的机会。”移民律师指出，过去这些设施处理今天出现的这些人数量时并没有问题。所以人们只能在外面等待，自生自灭。

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:55.010
<v Ira Glass>Here in Tijuana, it's so many people that they wait for weeks. So to keep things orderly, they have this notebook. And because it raises legal issues under asylum law for either the Mexican or American governments to keep a list like this, it's managed by an asylum seeker.
<v Ira Glass>在蒂华纳，人数太多，他们得等上几周。为了保持秩序，他们才有了这个笔记本。而且由于这类名单在墨西哥或美国政府手中保存会引发庇护法方面的法律问题，它是由一名寻求庇护者管理的。

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:57.940
<v Karen>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Karen>[讲西班牙语]

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:18.830
<v Ira Glass>The woman with the notebook is named Karen. She says that when she first arrived here from El Salvador, she put her name in the notebook-- her number is 598-- started paying for a cheap room nearby, and volunteered with Mexican officials to help clean the plaza, or whatever, just to be useful. And then at some point, the Jamaican refugee who'd been managing the notebook got his number called and passed the notebook to her.
<v Ira Glass>拿笔记本的女子叫卡伦。她说当她刚从萨尔瓦多抵达这里时，她把名字写进了本子——她的号码是598——开始在附近付钱租了一个便宜的房间，并自愿协助墨西哥官员清理广场什么的，总之是为了能做点有用的事。后来，之前负责管理笔记本的那位牙买加难民拿到了他的号码，就把笔记本传给了她。

00:04:18.830 --> 00:04:20.520
<v Karen>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Karen>[讲西班牙语]

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:35.090
<v Ira Glass>You really could not pick anybody better for this job. Karen managed people in her old job in the Salvadoran Ministry of Health, and says she was also a leader at her church. And she's good at managing people. When she's not shouting names, she calls everybody "preciosa," precious.
<v Ira Glass>你真的找不到比她更适合这份工作的人了。卡伦在萨尔瓦多卫生部工作时曾负责管理人员，她说她还是她所在教会的领袖。她很擅长管理人们。当她不喊名字时，她管每个人叫“preciosa”，意为“宝贝”。

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:36.900
<v Karen>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Karen>[讲西班牙语]

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:43.340
<v Ira Glass>And goes about the daily task of disappointing people whose numbers aren't picked with as much kindness as anybody could possibly muster.
<v Ira Glass>她每天都在尽可能温柔地去应对那些号码没被叫到的人，安慰他们。

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:47.630
<v Karen>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Karen>[讲西班牙语]

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:53.520
<v Ira Glass>I'm going to ask you all a favor, she's saying, but she doesn't say right away what it is. Dozens of tired people look at her.
<v Ira Glass>我请求大家帮个忙，她说，但她没有立刻说是什么忙。几十个疲惫的人看着她。

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.850
<v Karen>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Karen>[讲西班牙语]

00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:58.580
<v Ira Glass>I want us all to be happy for those who are going in.
<v Ira Glass>我希望我们都能为那些能够进入的人感到高兴。

00:04:58.580 --> 00:04:59.450
<v Karen>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Karen>[讲西班牙语]

00:04:59.450 --> 00:04:59.720
<v Ira Glass>Yeah?
<v Ira Glass>是吗？

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:04.430
<v Karen>[SPEAKING SPANISH] OK?
<v Karen>[讲西班牙语] 好吗？

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:06.920
<v Ira Glass>Because just like you, I'm waiting for my number, OK?
<v Ira Glass>因为就像你们一样，我也在等我的号码，好吗？

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:11.640
<v Karen>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Karen>[讲西班牙语]

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:24.110
<v Ira Glass>I know the effort to be here has been hard for everybody. I know this is really difficult. I understand because I'm living it, too. I'm also paying rent. I'm also spending money on food. But I want to appeal to your conscience, OK?
<v Ira Glass>我知道来到这里对每个人来说都不容易。我知道这真的很难。我理解，因为我也在经历这一切。我也在交房租。我也在花钱买食物。但我想要唤起你们的良知，好吗？

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:27.650
<v Karen>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Karen>[讲西班牙语]

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:39.890
<v Ira Glass>And here finally, she gets to the favor. She explains there's a pregnant woman she wants to jump to the front of the line, and she wants everybody be OK with that and feel good about it. Nobody seems too happy with this, but nobody complains, either.
<v Ira Glass>最后，她终于说出了这个请求。她解释说有一位孕妇，她想让对方排到队伍最前面，她希望大家都能同意并对此感到欣慰。没人看起来很高兴，但也没有人抱怨。

00:05:39.890 --> 00:05:45.750
<v Karen>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Karen>[讲西班牙语]

00:05:45.750 --> 00:06:05.310
<v Ira Glass>The person who brought me to see this is an LA Times reporter who first wrote about the existence of the notebook, Cindy Carcamo. Although the notebook is supposedly the system that asylum seekers are running for each other to make their wait more orderly, Cindy says they probably didn't create the notebook. In fact, most everything about the notebook is controlled by a Mexican government agency called Grupos Beta.
<v Ira Glass>带我来看这个场景的是《洛杉矶时报》记者辛迪·卡卡莫，她是最先写到这个笔记本存在的人。虽然笔记本被认为是寻求庇护者为彼此制定的让等待更有序的系统，但辛迪说这个笔记本很可能不是他们自己创建的。事实上，关于笔记本的一切几乎都由一个叫“贝塔小组”（Grupos Beta）的墨西哥政府机构控制。

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:15.130
<v Ira Glass>Grupos Beta takes the notebook each night and stores it in a safe. And a Grupos Beta official explained to Cindy and me that every morning, they talk to somebody from US Customs and Border Protection.
<v Ira Glass>贝塔小组每天晚上都会拿走笔记本并存放在保险箱里。一位贝塔小组的官员向我和辛迪解释说，他们每天早上都会与美国海关及边境保护局的人沟通。

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:16.230
<v Cindy Carcamo>[SPEAKING SPANISH]? Or no.
<v Cindy Carcamo>[讲西班牙语]？还是不。

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:17.490
<v Man>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Man>[讲西班牙语]

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:34.850
<v Cindy Carcamo>They just meet, talking. And then he says we're taking this many people in today, and that's how it works. Every day in the morning, US Customs and Border Protection gives a number to Mexican officials, and then the Mexican officials give that number to the notebook keeper and she calls that many names.
<v Cindy Carcamo>他们只是见面沟通。然后他说今天我们要收多少人，事情就是这样运作的。每天早上，美国海关及边境保护局给墨西哥官员一个数字，然后墨西哥官员把这个数字给笔记本管理员，她就叫多少个名字。

00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:50.660
<v Ira Glass>On this particular day, only 20 people were allowed to enter the port of entry. One day earlier in the week, it was zero. On any given day, with over 1,000 names uncalled in the notebook, it's 20, or 30, or 40. 50's the most anybody had seen.
<v Ira Glass>在这一天，只有20人被允许进入入境口岸。本周早些时候有一天是零。在任何一天，笔记本上有超过1000个名字没被叫到，但也只能进20、30或40人。50人是任何人见过的最多人数。

00:06:50.660 --> 00:07:09.660
<v Ira Glass>Cindy and I talked to lots of people who were waiting, and they were pretty much exactly who you'd expect. Many of them said they were escaping violence in Mexico or Central America. Some seemed truly terrified, even here in the plaza. One woman said that yesterday, her son thought that he'd spotted one of the men who was after them at the shelter they were staying at, so they fled to a hotel.
<v Ira Glass>我和辛迪与许多等待的人聊过，他们基本就是你所预期的那样。许多人说他们正在逃离墨西哥或中美洲的暴力。有些人看起来真的很恐惧，即使是在广场上。一位妇女说，昨天，她的儿子在他们寄宿的收容所似乎发现了追杀他们的人中的一个，所以他们逃到了一家旅馆。

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:26.310
<v Ira Glass>But we also met a 29-year-old mom named Lara, from a tiny Mexican town called Jalapa in Guerrero, who is here because her cousin in California keeps telling her that her kids would have a better life in the United States. So she sold off everything and brought the five of them here-- really nice kids, by the way-- hoping for political asylum.
<v Ira Glass>但我们也遇到了一位29岁的妈妈，叫拉拉，来自格雷罗州一个叫哈拉帕的小镇，她在这里是因为她在加州的堂姐一直告诉她，她的孩子在美国会有更好的生活。所以她卖掉了一切，把五个孩子带到了这里——顺便说一下，孩子们真的很可爱——希望能获得政治庇护。

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:28.570
<v Cindy Carcamo>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Cindy Carcamo>[讲西班牙语]

00:07:28.570 --> 00:07:28.790
<v Woman>No.
<v Woman>没有。

00:07:28.790 --> 00:07:31.060
<v Cindy Carcamo>No. No violence, she said. It's not about violence.
<v Cindy Carcamo>没有。没有暴力，她说。这不是关于暴力的问题。

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:55.850
<v Ira Glass>If she's not fleeing violence, she doesn't really have much chance of getting asylum in the United States. It was weird to talk to somebody and know that she probably would not get what she had pinned all her hopes on and traveled 1,500 miles for, and that she and her kids were probably going to wait for weeks and then get turned back. I never had the experience of knowing so clearly someone's future that they do not know. It was hard to keep looking her in the eye.
<v Ira Glass>如果她不是在逃离暴力，她在申请美国庇护方面其实没什么机会。和一个可能不会得到她倾注所有希望、跋涉1500英里想要的东西的人交谈，知道她和她的孩子们很可能要等上几周然后被遣返，这感觉很奇怪。我从未如此清晰地预见到别人的未来，而他们自己却不知道。看着她的眼睛很难受。

00:07:55.850 --> 00:08:01.420
<v Man>You know, everything was in Spanish, right? So I don't understand Spanish. I didn't get anything. So I want to know what's happening.
<v Man>你知道，一切都是西班牙语，对吧？我不懂西班牙语。我什么也没听懂。所以我很想知道发生了什么。

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:02.140
<v Karen>Give me a moment.
<v Karen>给我一点时间。

00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:06.220
<v Man>Yesterday you told me "manana." Today again, "manana."
<v Man>昨天你告诉我“明天”。今天又是“明天”。

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:06.860
<v Karen>OK.
<v Karen>好。

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:20.960
<v Ira Glass>When Karen finished rounding up her 20 people for the day, this guy from Cameroon, named Ransom, approached her. Karen's English doesn't go much past "give me a moment," and his Spanish doesn't go much past "manana." So Karen dragged in Cindy, the LA Times reporter, to translate.
<v Ira Glass>当卡伦忙完一天20人的名额后，这个叫兰索姆的喀麦隆人走近了她。卡伦的英语水平超不过“给我一点时间”，而他的西班牙语水平超不过“明天”。于是卡伦拉上《洛杉矶时报》记者辛迪来翻译。

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:24.400
<v Man>But can you ask her how many people will be waiting tomorrow?
<v Man>但你能问问她明天会有多少人排队吗？

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:27.360
<v Cindy Carcamo>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Cindy Carcamo>[讲西班牙语]

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:30.120
<v Karen>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Karen>[讲西班牙语]

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:48.190
<v Ira Glass>When they're done, Ransom told me and Cindy that he left Cameroon with 16 other people three months before. They flew to Ecuador, which is a path that lots of people take because visas are easy to get there. Then they walked through the jungle, north through Central America, sleeping in the bush, till they got here three weeks ago.
<v Ira Glass>交谈结束后，兰索姆告诉我和辛迪，他三个月前和另外16个人离开了喀麦隆。他们飞往厄瓜多尔，那是许多人选择的路径，因为那里的签证很容易拿到。然后他们穿过丛林，一路向北穿过中美洲，睡在灌木丛里，直到三周前到达这里。

00:08:48.190 --> 00:08:59.640
<v Man>It's been a tough journey. It's really been tough. It's been a long way. The money is finished. Like, I don't have a way to stay. That's the problem. That's why I'm frustrated. That's the problem.
<v Man>这是一段艰难的旅程。真的很艰难。路途遥远。钱花光了。比如，我没有办法留下来。这就是问题所在。这就是为什么我感到沮丧。这就是问题。

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:01.460
<v Cindy Carcamo>Where are you staying?
<v Cindy Carcamo>你住在哪里？

00:09:01.460 --> 00:09:12.030
<v Man>We just live nearby. We just-- you wouldn't understand. It's quite tough. We're like 40 people in one room. One small room. It's just-- I don't know how something like that--
<v Man>我们就住在附近。我们只是——你不会明白的。这相当艰难。我们大概40个人挤在一个房间里。一个小房间。这只是——我不知道那种情况怎么会——

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:13.230
<v Ira Glass>How much do you pay for that?
<v Ira Glass>你每月付多少钱？

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:15.520
<v Man>We pay like $50 a month.
<v Man>我们一个月付大约50美元。

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:27.440
<v Ira Glass>And he's going to be here so long that he has to pay a second month. He says they were robbed along the way in the jungle. So when he arrived in Tijuana three weeks ago, he only had $80. With so little money, how is he surviving?
<v Ira Glass>他在这里呆的时间太长了，以至于必须付第二个月的房租。他说他们在丛林中被抢劫了。所以当他三周前到达蒂华纳时，他只有80美元。只有这么点钱，他是怎么活下来的？

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:35.210
<v Man>Well, water in the absence of food. I drink water.
<v Man>嗯，在没有食物的情况下喝水。我喝水。

00:09:35.210 --> 00:09:46.710
<v Ira Glass>The situation he's fleeing should make him a good candidate for asylum in the United States. The government of Cameroon has been killing the English-speaking minority in the country and burning their homes, trying to suppress an armed uprising.
<v Ira Glass>他逃离的处境本应让他成为美国庇护的理想候选人。喀麦隆政府一直在杀害国内讲英语的少数群体并烧毁他们的房屋，试图镇压武装起义。

00:09:46.710 --> 00:10:05.160
<v Ira Glass>But it's a hard moment to try to enter our country. The Trump administration is doing all kinds of things to be stricter with potential immigrants. And as you'll hear this hour, some of them are keeping desperate people waiting outside the border for weeks. Even if you understand that we're trying to be stricter with who's coming into the country, it can be hard to understand why we're doing it like this.
<v Ira Glass>但现在是尝试进入我们国家的艰难时刻。特朗普政府正在采取各种措施对潜在移民更加严厉。正如你在这个小时里听到的，其中一些措施让绝望的人在边境之外等待数周。即使你理解我们试图对进入该国的人员实施更严格的限制，也很难理解我们为什么要以这种方式行事。

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:23.760
<v Ira Glass>Today on our program, I think at this point, most of us have heard about a few of the things that the President and his appointees have done to try to get control of the border. We've heard about the family separations, we've heard about the travel ban. But in fact, it's a wide-ranging, coordinated effort across many agencies. All kinds of things that have not gotten a lot of attention.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的节目中，我想此时大多数人都已经听说过总统及其任命的官员为试图控制边境所采取的一些措施。我们听说了家庭分离，听说了旅行禁令。但实际上，这是一项跨越多个机构的广泛、协调的努力。各种各样没有得到太多关注的事情。

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:49.170
<v Ira Glass>What's interesting is that so much of their effort, lots of the things that they're doing, are not cracking down on people breaking the law or sneaking across the border, not targeting criminals or bad hombres. Many of the efforts are targeted at the people who are trying their best to play by the rules and do what we ask of foreigners who want to come here. People trying to obey our laws, like asylum seekers showing up at our door saying, I'm in danger at home. Your laws allow me to apply for haven.
<v Ira Glass>有趣的是，他们的许多努力、他们正在做的很多事情，并不是打击违法或潜入边境的人，也不是针对罪犯或坏分子。许多努力针对的是那些竭尽全力遵守规则、按照我们要求向那些想要来这里的人行事的人。那些试图遵守我们法律的人，比如出现在我们门口说“我在家乡处于危险中，你们的法律允许我申请避难”的寻求庇护者。

00:10:49.170 --> 00:11:04.350
<v Ira Glass>Today we take you on a tour of the many, many things that are all happening, the ways that we're shutting our doors to outsiders. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天我们将带你参观正在发生的所有事情，以及我们拒之门外的方式。来自WBEZ芝加哥，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请留下。

00:11:04.350 --> 00:11:33.190
<v Ira Glass>Act One, All Together Now. So to enter the United States from the south, you can go to the official border station, the port of entry, and do it legally, like the people who you heard trying to do it in that plaza in Tijuana. Well, you might get to that plaza, see the line, see the wait, and pause, or maybe try to go to the border station in Brownsville or El Paso. Again, you might pause and decide to take your kids, and take your chances, and head out to cross the border illegally.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，大家一起。所以，要从南部进入美国，你可以去官方边境站，也就是入境口岸，合法进入，就像你在蒂华纳广场听到的那些人试图做的那样。嗯，你可能会到达那个广场，看到排队，看到等待，然后停下来，或者可能尝试去布朗斯维尔或埃尔帕索的边境站。同样，你可能会犹豫，决定带上孩子，碰碰运气，然后非法跨越边境。

00:11:33.190 --> 00:11:44.570
<v Ira Glass>I was talking to a Customs and Border Protection officer, a spokesman for the union, Chris Cabrera. He patrols the area around the Rio Grande and he says that he picks up people like this all the time. They tell him they tried the port of entry.
<v Ira Glass>我当时正在和一名海关及边境保护局官员、工会发言人克里斯·卡布雷拉交谈。他在里奥格兰德河周边巡逻，他说他一直在抓捕这样的人。他们告诉他，他们尝试过入境口岸。

00:11:44.570 --> 00:11:53.980
<v Chris Cabrera>The line was too long. They didn't want to wait that long. And that's why a lot of them choose to come through the river, because they don't want to wait.
<v Chris Cabrera>队伍太长了。他们不想等那么久。这就是为什么他们中的很多人选择穿过河流，因为他们不想等。

00:11:53.980 --> 00:12:13.770
<v Ira Glass>Once he apprehends them, there's something new now that they go through under this administration. A new thing that's happening on the border. People caught crossing into the country illegally are being rounded up and sent to federal criminal court in mass hearings with dozens of people getting sentenced at the same time. The government has done these hearings in the past, but never on the scale that's happening now.
<v Ira Glass>一旦他抓获他们，现在他们在这个政府下经历了一些新的东西。边境上发生的一件新事。被抓到非法进入该国的人正在被聚集起来，送往联邦刑事法庭进行大规模听证会，几十人同时被判刑。政府过去也做过这种听证会，但规模从未达到现在的程度。

00:12:13.770 --> 00:12:36.250
<v Ira Glass>As of April, they're doing them in every district along the border, sometimes twice a day, with tens of thousands of people convicted. They're prosecuting a crime that didn't used to be treated like a crime. Before this, if you cross the border illegally, you'd most likely just get put into immigration proceedings and probably deported. Now, you go through criminal court first, even if you came here to ask for asylum.
<v Ira Glass>截至4月，他们在边境沿线的所有地区都在进行这种听证，有时一天两次，有数万人被定罪。他们正在起诉一种过去不被视为犯罪的行为。在此之前，如果你非法越境，你最有可能只是进入移民程序并可能被驱逐出境。现在，你首先要经历刑事法庭，即使你来到这里是为了申请庇护。

00:12:36.250 --> 00:12:53.580
<v Ira Glass>This is happening under the administration's Zero Tolerance Policy. You probably heard about the part of that policy that separated children from their parents. That part has officially ended. But this move to prosecute border crossings as criminal offenses, this was actually the main point of the Zero Tolerance Policy.
<v Ira Glass>这是在政府的“零容忍政策”下发生的。你可能听说过该政策中将儿童与父母分离的那部分。那部分已经正式结束。但这一将非法越境作为刑事犯罪起诉的举动，实际上才是“零容忍政策”的重点。

00:12:53.580 --> 00:13:09.660
<v Ira Glass>Reporter Julia Preston went to McAllen, Texas, to one of the courts that's had the highest number of prosecutions since this all started to see what this looks like in practice. And what she discovered is what's happening now is just the first step towards much bigger things that are in the works. Here's Julia.
<v Ira Glass>记者朱莉娅·普雷斯顿去了德克萨斯州的麦卡伦，前往自这一切开始以来起诉案件数量最高的法院之一，看看这在实践中是什么样子。她发现，现在正在发生的事情只是正在酝酿的更大事情的第一步。以下是朱莉娅的报道。

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:30.720
<v Julia Preston>Picture a federal courtroom. The defendants sit with their lawyers at one table, the prosecutors on the other side, the judge up front. The McAllen courtroom was a stunningly different site. Five rows of wooden benches, usually meant for visitors, were packed with defendants, squeezed in shoulder to shoulder. 74 of them.
<v Julia Preston>想象一下联邦法庭。被告和他们的律师坐在一张桌子旁，检察官在另一侧，法官坐在前面。麦卡伦法庭是一个截然不同的场景。五排通常供访客使用的木制长椅上挤满了被告，肩膀挨着肩膀。总共74人。

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:49.070
<v Julia Preston>The defendants were dressed in tattered t-shirts and jeans, not prison uniforms. Some of the men had trouble keeping their pants up when they stood because their belts were taken away when they were detained. They'd been caught by the Border Patrol after they crossed the Rio Grande River. Most of them just one or two days before.
<v Julia Preston>被告穿着破旧的T恤和牛仔裤，而不是囚服。有些男人站立时不得不提着裤子，因为他们的腰带在被拘留时被没收了。他们在越过里奥格兰德河后被边境巡逻队抓获。他们中的大多数人就在一两天前被抓。

00:13:49.070 --> 00:13:57.540
<v Scott Hacker>Ladies and gentlemen, let me ask you please to stand and raise your right hand. Take an oath before the court this morning for these proceedings.
<v Scott Hacker>女士们、先生们，请允许我请你们站起来并举起右手。在今天早上的这些诉讼程序前进行宣誓。

00:13:57.540 --> 00:14:10.590
<v Julia Preston>That's the sound of 74 people standing up to be sworn in when they're shackled at the ankles and chained around the waist. They all had one hand that was cuffed tightly to the waist chain and just one hand free.
<v Julia Preston>那是74人站起来宣誓的声音，他们的脚踝戴着脚镣，腰间缠着铁链。他们每个人都有一只手紧紧地铐在腰链上，只有一只手是自由的。

00:14:10.590 --> 00:14:29.400
<v Julia Preston>In another hearing I went to, there were 87 defendants. In that one, the marshals had to put some of them in the jury box. The defendants would all be out of there-- charged, convicted, and sentenced-- in just over two hours. It was mass justice at head-spinning speed.
<v Julia Preston>在我去的另一场听证会上，有87名被告。在那场听证会上，法警不得不把其中一些人安排在陪审团席上。这些被告——被起诉、定罪、判刑——将在两个多小时内全部结束离开。这是以令人目眩的速度进行的大规模司法审判。

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:52.530
<v Julia Preston>The magistrate judge presiding over this hearing, Scott Hacker, didn't sit in his usual place on the high bench. Instead, he spent the whole hearing on his feet, right in front of the migrants, so he could see each one of them. The migrants were wearing wireless headphones to hear the Spanish interpreter. Judge Hacker took a few minutes to make sure everyone's headphone was working.
<v Julia Preston>主持这场听证会的治安法官斯科特·哈克没有坐在他平时高高的法官席上。相反，他在整个听证期间都站着，就在移民们面前，这样他就能看清每一个人。移民们戴着无线耳机收听西班牙语翻译。哈克法官花了几分钟确保每个人的耳机都能正常工作。

00:14:52.530 --> 00:14:56.730
<v Scott Hacker>Sir, can you hear? The gentlemen next to you. Can you hear?
<v Scott Hacker>先生，你能听见吗？你旁边的先生。你能听见吗？

00:14:56.730 --> 00:15:25.660
<v Julia Preston>The defendants awkwardly adjusted their headphones with their one free hand. Throughout the hearing, the security guards ran around as tech support, resetting the headphones or swapping them out for fresh ones. Judge Hacker ran the hearing like a professor, explaining things in plain language and raising an index finger in the air in big gestures to punctuate his points. He addressed the migrants as sir and ma'am, and ladies and gentlemen.
<v Julia Preston>被告们用他们唯一自由的手笨拙地调整着耳机。在整个听证过程中，保安人员像技术支持一样跑来跑去，重置耳机或换上新的。哈克法官像教授一样主持听证会，用通俗易懂的语言解释事情，并用食指在空中比划大动作来强调他的重点。他称呼移民们为“先生”和“女士”，以及“女士们、先生们”。

00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:32.010
<v Scott Hacker>This is your day in court. It's important that you hear and understand everything that I explain to you.
<v Scott Hacker>这是你们在法庭上的日子。你们听到并理解我解释的一切非常重要。

00:15:32.010 --> 00:16:04.590
<v Julia Preston>I'll admit, I was surprised. I was expecting some kind of rodeo justice. People herded through with no idea what was happening to them. But it wasn't that. The judge, the public defenders, even the prosecutors seemed to be working hard to ensure that all 74 defendants got some kind of basic due process. Every time the judge explained something, he would go around pointing to each defendant, one by one, getting them to confirm that they understood.
<v Julia Preston>我承认，我感到惊讶。我原本预想的是某种“牛仔正义”。人们被成群地赶过去，根本不知道在他们身上发生了什么。但事实并非如此。法官、公设辩护律师，甚至检察官似乎都在努力确保所有74名被告都能获得某种基本的正当程序。法官每次解释一些事情，他都会绕场一周，一个个指着每位被告，让他们确认他们理解了。

00:16:04.590 --> 00:16:15.880
<v Scott Hacker>Then I'll start with the person on the front row. [INAUDIBLE]. He'll answer yes or no, then I'll point to Miss [INAUDIBLE]. She'll answer. Then I'll point to Miss Perez, she'll answer.
<v Scott Hacker>那么我从第一排的那个人开始。[听不清]。他会回答是或否，然后我指向[听不清]女士。她会回答。然后我指向佩雷斯女士，她会回答。

00:16:15.880 --> 00:16:27.510
<v Scott Hacker>When I'm finished with the first row, I'll start with the gentleman on the second row. The third row, the fourth row, and continue until I get everyone. Have you understood the instructions? Yes or no, sir. Next.
<v Scott Hacker>当我结束第一排后，我将从第二排的绅士开始。第三排，第四排，并继续直到每个人都结束。你们理解指令了吗？是或否，先生。下一位。

